  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cyclone Remal Recovery Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the wake of Cyclone Remal&apos;s devastating landfall in Satkhira district on Sunday evening, over 3.75 million people were directly affected across 19 districts with over 150,000 houses damaged. Adding to the challenges, parts of southern districts experienced prolonged power outages lasting over 36 hours. Around 27 million subscribers of Bangladesh Rural Electrification Board were left without electricity, exacerbating the difficulties faced by affected communities. Communication networks were severely impacted, with 15,000 telecom towers disrupted, leaving millions without mobile network access. The cyclone also dealt a significant blow to the livelihoods of coastal communities, particularly in Khulna, where approximately 8,875 shrimp enclosures were washed away, resulting in an estimated financial loss of USD 40 million for fish farmers. According to the Department of Agriculture Extension, in Khulna district alone, crops on 12,715 hectares of land were inundated. 

In response to the devastation left behind by the cyclone, the Remal Recovery Response project aims to provide immediate relief and long-term support to those affected by Cyclone Remal. Through collaborative partnerships and targeted interventions, the project seeks to address the urgent needs of impacted communities and pave the way for their recovery and resilience in the most affected regions that falls within the Cordaid&#x2019;s working area, namely &#x2013; Sharankhola, Rampal upazila of Bagerhat district, and Kalapara of Pautakhali district.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The objective of the proposed project is to provide disaster affected vulnerable community with life saving measures and provide options to restore their livelihoods.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2022;	Total beneficiaries: 1645 households (13,195 total participants - on average 4.13 persons in each household, BBS 2022 data)
&#x2022;	Inclusion target: At least 50% Female, 10% elderly and 2% participants with disability of the total project participants</narrative>
  </description>
